{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"16cd228c-b257-446d-b43b-c6a9d829b6cd","title":"Doppler effect with circular wavefronts"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"16cd228c-b257-446d-b43b-c6a9d829b6cd","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A moving source emits successive circular wavefronts. Each circle expands from where the source was when that wave was emitted.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Doppler effect: moving source"},{"op":"figure","id":"waves","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-7,5],"y":[-5,5],"axes":"none","equal_scale":true},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"oldwave","center":[-1.5,0],"r":4.5},{"kind":"circle","id":"midwave","center":[-1,0],"r":3},{"kind":"circle","id":"newwave","center":[-0.5,0],"r":1.5},{"kind":"point","at":[-1.5,0]},{"kind":"point","at":[-1,0]},{"kind":"point","at":[-0.5,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"source","at":[0,0],"label":"source"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"source"},"to":[0.8,0],"arrow":true},{"kind":"text","at":[0,-4.4],"text":"motion →"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"conditions","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"0<v_s<c,\\qquad T=\\frac{1}{f_s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"16cd228c-b257-446d-b43b-c6a9d829b6cd","step":{"id":"s2","script":"During one emission period, the source advances. Wavefronts bunch together ahead and spread apart behind; the sound speed itself stays unchanged.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"spacing","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[1,0],"to":[2,0],"label":"λ ahead"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-4,0],"to":[-2,0],"label":"λ behind"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"frontlambda","anchor":{"el":"conditions","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\lambda_{\\text{ahead}}=(c-v_s)T"},{"op":"equation","id":"backlambda","anchor":{"el":"frontlambda","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\lambda_{\\text{behind}}=(c+v_s)T"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"16cd228c-b257-446d-b43b-c6a9d829b6cd","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Stationary listeners hear higher frequency ahead and lower frequency behind. More closely spaced wavefronts pass a listener more often.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"listeners","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":[4,0],"label":"higher pitch"},{"kind":"point","at":[-6.5,0],"label":"lower pitch"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"frontfreq","anchor":{"el":"backlambda","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f_{\\text{ahead}}=\\frac{c}{\\lambda_{\\text{ahead}}}=f_s\\frac{c}{c-v_s}>f_s"},{"op":"equation","id":"backfreq","anchor":{"el":"frontfreq","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f_{\\text{behind}}=f_s\\frac{c}{c+v_s}<f_s"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the source moves faster but remains slower than sound, what happens to the pitch heard ahead?","expected_gist":"It increases because the wavefront spacing ahead decreases."}}}
